<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagavata Purana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Srimad Bhagavatam, Srimad Bhagavata Mahapurana, Bhagavata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhagavata Purana, also known as the Srimad Bhagavatam, is one of the eighteen major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> (Mahapuranas) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and a central text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. Composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and traditionally attributed to Veda Vyasa, it promotes bhakti, devotion, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and treats cosmology, genealogy, legend and yoga. Its twelve books hold 335 chapters and 18,000 verses; the tenth, with about 4,000 verses, has been the most popular and widely studied. Scholars date it variously, many placing much of it in the Gupta period and others its final redaction in the 9th or early 10th century. It was the first Purana translated into a European language, in a French version of a Tamil text in 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana.jpg" alt="Bhagavata Purana" width="330" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajasthan_manuscript_page_of_Bhagavata_Purana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
